    {
      "id": 23,
      "title": "Implement E2E Tests for Security Tool Plugins (HttpX, Nmap, and Others)",
      "description": "Create comprehensive end-to-end tests for security tool plugins to verify the complete scan-to-results workflow functions correctly through the UI.",
      "details": "Develop end-to-end tests for security tool plugins including HttpX, Nmap, and other security plugins. The tests should cover the following aspects:\n\n1. **Test Setup**:\n   - Create mock services/targets that can be safely scanned\n   - Configure appropriate test environments with the security tools installed\n   - Implement test fixtures that can reset the application state between tests\n\n2. **UI Interaction Tests**:\n   - Verify that scan configuration options are correctly displayed\n   - Test that scan triggers work through the UI (button clicks, form submissions)\n   - Ensure proper validation of scan parameters\n   - Test cancellation functionality if applicable\n\n3. **JobsTracker Integration**:\n   - Verify jobs appear in the JobsTracker immediately after triggering\n   - Test that progress updates are displayed correctly\n   - Ensure status changes (queued, running, completed, failed) are reflected accurately\n   - Test that job details can be accessed from the tracker\n\n4. **Results Processing**:\n   - Verify scan results are correctly parsed and stored\n   - Test that results appear in service enrichments section\n   - Ensure all expected data fields are populated\n   - Verify any visualizations or special formatting of results\n\n5. **Error Handling**:\n   - Test behavior when scans fail for various reasons\n   - Verify appropriate error messages are displayed\n   - Test recovery mechanisms if applicable\n\nImplement these tests using the project's preferred E2E testing framework (like Cypress, Playwright, or Selenium). Structure the tests to be maintainable and resilient to UI changes by using proper selectors and page object patterns.",
      "testStrategy": "The E2E tests themselves should be validated using the following approach:\n\n1. **Manual Verification**:\n   - Initially run each test manually while observing the application to ensure the test correctly simulates user behavior\n   - Verify that assertions match expected application behavior\n\n2. **Test Coverage Analysis**:\n   - Ensure tests cover all critical paths in the scan workflow\n   - Verify tests for both successful and failure scenarios\n   - Confirm edge cases are handled (large scans, empty results, etc.)\n\n3. **Reliability Testing**:\n   - Run the test suite multiple times to ensure consistency\n   - Verify tests are not flaky or dependent on timing issues\n   - Test in different environments if applicable\n\n4. **Specific Test Cases**:\n   - HttpX plugin: Test URL scanning, parameter validation, and result display\n   - Nmap plugin: Test network scanning, port discovery, and service identification\n   - For each plugin: Test with minimal configuration and with advanced options\n   - Test concurrent execution of multiple security scans\n   - Verify proper cleanup after tests complete\n\n5. **Integration with CI/CD**:\n   - Ensure tests can run in the CI/CD pipeline\n   - Verify reporting of test results is clear and actionable\n   - Set up appropriate screenshots or video recording for failed tests\n\nThe tests should be considered successful when they consistently verify the entire workflow from scan configuration through to results display without false positives or negatives.",
