    {
      "id": 27,
      "title": "Implement Parallel E2E Test Execution Framework",
      "description": "Develop a system for concurrent execution of end-to-end tests with Docker container orchestration, test queue management, and integration with JobsTracker.",
      "details": "Create a parallel test execution framework with the following components:\n\n1. **Docker Container Orchestration**:\n   - Implement a container manager that can spawn and manage multiple browser instances in Docker containers\n   - Create a dynamic scaling mechanism based on available system resources\n   - Ensure proper cleanup of containers after test completion or failure\n\n2. **Test Queue Management**:\n   - Develop a queue system to manage test execution order and priority\n   - Implement mechanisms to handle test dependencies (tests that must run sequentially)\n   - Create a dispatcher that assigns tests to available containers\n\n3. **Resource Allocation**:\n   - Implement configurable limits for maximum concurrent tests\n   - Add resource monitoring to prevent system overload\n   - Create a throttling mechanism for when system resources are constrained\n\n4. **Test Isolation**:\n   - Ensure tests running in parallel don't interfere with each other\n   - Implement data isolation strategies (separate test databases or schemas)\n   - Add mechanisms to prevent race conditions in shared resources\n\n5. **JobsTracker Integration**:\n   - Extend the JobsTracker API to support multiple concurrent test jobs\n   - Add real-time status updates for all running tests\n   - Implement aggregated reporting for test results\n\n6. **Configuration**:\n   - Create configuration options for controlling parallelism level\n   - Add ability to specify which tests can run in parallel and which must run sequentially\n   - Implement environment-specific settings (dev/staging/production)\n\nThe implementation should be optimized for both speed and stability, with appropriate error handling and recovery mechanisms.",
      "testStrategy": "Testing should verify both functionality and performance of the parallel execution framework:\n\n1. **Functional Testing**:\n   - Verify correct execution of tests in parallel (create a set of 20+ sample tests that run successfully in parallel)\n   - Confirm test isolation by creating tests that would interfere if not properly isolated\n   - Test error scenarios (container failure, resource exhaustion) to ensure proper recovery\n   - Validate that test dependencies are respected (sequential tests run in correct order)\n\n2. **Performance Testing**:\n   - Measure execution time improvements compared to sequential execution\n   - Benchmark with different parallelism levels (2, 4, 8, 16 concurrent tests)\n   - Test resource utilization under various loads\n   - Verify system stability during extended test runs (100+ tests)\n\n3. **Integration Testing**:\n   - Confirm JobsTracker correctly displays all concurrent test jobs\n   - Verify real-time updates are working for all parallel tests\n   - Test that aggregated reporting correctly combines results from all tests\n\n4. **Edge Cases**:\n   - Test behavior when maximum resource limits are reached\n   - Verify system handles tests with varying durations appropriately\n   - Confirm proper cleanup after abnormal termination (kill -9)\n\nDocument performance improvements with metrics comparing sequential vs parallel execution times for the test suite.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        26
      ],
      "priority": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": 28,
      "title": "Create Comprehensive E2E Testing Documentation",
      "description": "Develop complete documentation for the E2E testing system that follows PenPal's existing documentation patterns and covers all aspects from test creation to troubleshooting.",
      "details": "Create a comprehensive documentation suite for the E2E testing framework with the following components:\n\n1. **Plugin Developer Guide**:\n   - Step-by-step instructions for registering new tests\n   - Integration points with the existing testing framework\n   - Configuration options and parameters\n   - Lifecycle hooks and events\n\n2. **Test Writing Best Practices**:\n   - Code organization and structure\n   - Naming conventions\n   - Handling asynchronous operations\n   - Managing test data and state\n   - Isolation techniques\n   - Performance considerations\n\n3. **API Reference Documentation**:\n   - Complete documentation of all available methods, classes, and interfaces\n   - Parameter descriptions and return types\n   - Usage examples for each API component\n   - Version compatibility information\n\n4. **Troubleshooting Guide**:\n   - Common error scenarios and their solutions\n   - Debugging techniques\n   - Performance optimization tips\n   - Environment-specific considerations\n\n5. **Example Test Implementations**:\n   - Basic test examples\n   - Complex scenarios with multiple components\n   - Mock and stub usage examples\n   - Data-driven test examples\n\nAll documentation should follow PenPal's existing documentation patterns for consistency, including formatting, terminology, and organization. Use the same documentation tooling and ensure cross-referencing between related sections. The documentation should be accessible both within the codebase and in a compiled format for the documentation portal.",
      "testStrategy": "The documentation should be validated through the following methods:\n\n1. **Technical Review**:\n   - Have at least two senior developers review the documentation for technical accuracy\n   - Verify all API references match the actual implementation\n   - Ensure all code examples are functional and follow best practices\n\n2. **Usability Testing**:\n   - Select 2-3 developers unfamiliar with the E2E testing system\n   - Assign them tasks that require using the documentation\n   - Collect feedback on clarity, completeness, and ease of use\n   - Document areas of confusion or missing information\n\n3. **Documentation Completeness Checklist**:\n   - Verify all required sections are present\n   - Confirm all API methods are documented\n   - Check that all examples are working and up-to-date\n   - Ensure troubleshooting covers common issues\n\n4. **Integration with Documentation System**:\n   - Verify the documentation builds correctly in PenPal's documentation system\n   - Check that all links, references, and navigation work properly\n   - Ensure proper formatting and readability on all supported devices\n\n5. **Practical Application Test**:\n   - Have a developer implement a new E2E test using only the documentation\n   - Document any points where additional information was needed",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        27
      ],
      "priority": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": 29,
      "title": "Implement Comprehensive Error Handling and Recovery System for E2E Tests",
      "description": "Develop a robust error handling framework for the E2E testing system that can gracefully manage browser crashes, timeouts, network failures, and ensure proper resource cleanup while providing detailed error reports.",
      "details": "Create a centralized error handling system that addresses the following scenarios:\n\n1. Browser Crash Recovery:\n   - Implement detection mechanisms for browser crashes during test execution\n   - Create recovery procedures to restart browser sessions automatically\n   - Preserve test state where possible to resume from checkpoint rather than restart\n   - Add configurable retry logic with exponential backoff\n\n2. Test Timeout Management:\n   - Implement tiered timeout system (global test suite, individual test, and operation-level timeouts)\n   - Add timeout detection with appropriate error messages identifying the specific operation that timed out\n   - Create hooks for custom timeout handling based on test context\n\n3. Network Failure Resilience:\n   - Add interceptors to detect network failures during API calls or page loads\n   - Implement configurable retry mechanisms for transient network issues\n   - Create mock responses for critical network dependencies when in fallback mode\n\n4. Resource Cleanup:\n   - Implement 'finally' blocks or equivalent cleanup mechanisms for all resource-intensive operations\n   - Create a resource registry to track opened connections, created files, or database transactions\n   - Develop an emergency cleanup routine that runs on test failure\n   - Add process hooks to ensure cleanup runs even during abnormal termination\n\n5. Error Reporting:\n   - Create structured error objects with standardized fields (error type, location, timestamp, context)\n   - Implement screenshot capture on failure with browser state information\n   - Add console log and network request capturing during failures\n   - Develop a reporting mechanism that aggregates error data into actionable reports\n\nThe implementation should use a modular approach allowing components to be used independently or as a complete system. All error handling should be non-intrusive to existing test code where possible.",
      "testStrategy": "Testing should verify the error handling system works correctly across all failure scenarios:\n\n1. Browser Crash Testing:\n   - Simulate browser crashes using process termination during test execution\n   - Verify the system detects the crash and attempts recovery\n   - Confirm tests can continue execution after recovery\n   - Validate that the correct error information is logged\n\n2. Timeout Testing:\n   - Create deliberately slow-responding test components\n   - Verify timeout detection at different levels (global, test, operation)\n   - Confirm timeout errors include specific information about what operation timed out\n   - Test that cleanup routines execute properly after timeouts\n\n3. Network Failure Testing:\n   - Use network throttling tools to simulate connection issues\n   - Create proxy servers that can simulate different types of network failures\n   - Verify retry mechanisms work correctly for transient issues\n   - Confirm permanent failures are reported clearly\n\n4. Resource Cleanup Validation:\n   - Instrument resource creation to track what should be cleaned up\n   - Force test failures at different stages and verify all resources are properly released\n   - Monitor for resource leaks across multiple test runs\n   - Test abnormal termination scenarios to ensure cleanup hooks execute\n\n5. Error Reporting Verification:\n   - Review error reports for completeness and clarity\n   - Verify screenshots are captured correctly on failures\n   - Confirm console logs and network requests are included in reports\n   - Test that error aggregation provides actionable information\n\nCreate a test matrix covering all combinations of failure types and test scenarios to ensure comprehensive coverage. Implement metrics to measure the effectiveness of the error handling system, such as recovery success rate and mean time to recovery.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        27
      ],
      "priority": "medium"
    },
    {
      "id": 30,
      "title": "Implement CI/CD Integration Framework for E2E Tests",
      "description": "Develop a comprehensive framework that enables seamless integration of end-to-end tests with CI/CD pipelines, supporting both local development and automated testing environments.",
      "details": "Create a modular integration framework that addresses the following components:\n\n1. Headless Execution Mode:\n   - Implement a command-line flag (--headless) that runs tests without UI\n   - Ensure browser automation works in headless environments\n   - Add logging enhancements for debugging headless runs\n\n2. Test Result Reporting:\n   - Develop exporters for common formats (JUnit XML, JSON, HTML)\n   - Include test duration, status, error messages, and screenshots\n   - Implement real-time reporting for long-running test suites\n\n3. CI System Integration:\n   - Configure proper exit codes (0 for success, non-zero for failures)\n   - Add environment detection to auto-configure for common CI systems (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, CircleCI, GitLab CI)\n   - Implement retry mechanisms for flaky tests with configurable thresholds\n\n4. Environment Configuration:\n   - Create a configuration system that supports different environments (dev, staging, production)\n   - Implement secure credential management for test environments\n   - Add configuration validation to prevent misconfiguration\n\n5. Local Development Support:\n   - Develop a watch mode for rapid test-code-test cycles\n   - Create simplified reporting for developer feedback\n   - Implement selective test running based on changed files\n\nThe framework should be implemented as a separate module that can be imported into the main test codebase. Documentation should include examples for all major CI systems and local development workflows.",
      "testStrategy": "Testing should verify all aspects of the CI/CD integration framework:\n\n1. Functional Testing:\n   - Verify headless mode works across all supported browsers\n   - Confirm all reporting formats contain accurate test results\n   - Test exit code behavior with passing, failing, and mixed test results\n   - Validate environment configuration loading for all target environments\n\n2. Integration Testing:\n   - Create mock CI environments for each supported system\n   - Run test suites in each environment and verify proper integration\n   - Test credential handling with mock secrets\n\n3. Performance Testing:\n   - Measure overhead added by the reporting mechanisms\n   - Benchmark headless vs. headed mode execution times\n   - Test with large test suites (100+ tests) to ensure scalability\n\n4. Usability Testing:\n   - Have developers use the local development features and gather feedback\n   - Verify documentation clarity with team members not involved in development\n\n5. Validation Scenarios:\n   - Simulate network failures during test reporting\n   - Test behavior when configuration files are malformed\n   - Verify retry mechanism correctly handles flaky tests\n\nCreate a dedicated test suite that can be run to verify the framework independently of the actual application tests. Include a CI workflow that tests the framework itself on each commit.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "priority": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "title": "Optimize E2E Testing Performance with Caching and Resource Management",
      "description": "Implement performance optimizations for the end-to-end testing system to reduce execution time while maintaining test reliability and isolation.",
      "details": "This task involves implementing several key optimizations to the E2E testing infrastructure:\n\n1. Docker Image Optimization:\n   - Configure Docker layer caching for test images\n   - Implement a caching strategy for base images\n   - Minimize image size by removing unnecessary dependencies\n   - Use multi-stage builds to reduce final image size\n\n2. Browser Instance Management:\n   - Implement browser instance pooling to reuse browser contexts between tests\n   - Configure parallel browser sessions with resource limits\n   - Add warm-up mechanisms to pre-initialize browser instances\n   - Implement proper cleanup of browser resources\n\n3. Test Execution Optimization:\n   - Analyze and optimize test boot time\n   - Implement parallel test execution where appropriate\n   - Add test sharding capabilities for distributing tests across runners\n   - Optimize test dependencies to minimize setup/teardown overhead\n\n4. Resource Monitoring:\n   - Add CPU/memory usage tracking during test runs\n   - Implement logging of test execution times\n   - Create dashboards for visualizing performance metrics\n   - Set up alerts for performance regressions\n\n5. Cleanup Automation:\n   - Implement automatic cleanup of test artifacts\n   - Add garbage collection for orphaned Docker containers/volumes\n   - Schedule periodic cleanup of cached resources\n\nThe implementation should include configuration options to balance between maximum performance and test isolation. Document all optimization strategies and their impact on test reliability.",
      "testStrategy": "Testing this optimization work should include:\n\n1. Benchmark Testing:\n   - Establish baseline metrics for current E2E test execution time\n   - Measure execution time before and after each optimization\n   - Run the full test suite 5 times and compare average execution times\n   - Document performance improvements with statistical significance\n\n2. Resource Usage Validation:\n   - Monitor and record CPU, memory, and disk usage during test runs\n   - Verify that resource consumption is reduced or maintained within acceptable limits\n   - Test under various system load conditions to ensure stability\n\n3. Reliability Testing:\n   - Verify that test results remain consistent after optimizations\n   - Run the test suite 10 consecutive times to ensure no flakiness is introduced\n   - Compare test failure rates before and after optimization\n\n4. Isolation Verification:\n   - Confirm that tests remain properly isolated despite resource sharing\n   - Validate that test data doesn't leak between test runs\n   - Verify that browser state is properly reset between tests\n\n5. Scalability Testing:\n   - Test performance with increasing numbers of parallel tests\n   - Measure resource usage at different concurrency levels\n   - Determine optimal parallelization settings\n\nDocument all metrics in a performance report that compares before and after states, with recommendations for further optimizations.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        30
      ],
      "priority": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": 32,
      "title": "Implement ICMP Ping Plugin for Network Host Discovery",
      "description": "Develop a containerized Ping plugin that performs fast ICMP sweeps to discover hosts in a network, integrating with PenPal's existing architecture and services.",
      "details": "Create a new Ping plugin that leverages ICMP protocol for efficient host discovery across network ranges. The plugin should:\n\n1. Be containerized using Docker with a Dockerfile that includes necessary dependencies for ICMP operations\n2. Subscribe to MQTT topics for new network discoveries (e.g., 'penpal/discoveries/networks')\n3. Upon receiving network information, queue scans using the ScanQueue service\n4. Implement fast ICMP sweep functionality that can handle various network sizes efficiently\n5. Track scan jobs using the JobsTracker service, updating status as appropriate\n6. Process scan results and upsert discovered hosts to CoreAPI\n7. Extend the GraphQL schema to support:\n   - Querying ping results by network, IP range, or specific hosts\n   - Checking scan status and history\n   - Retrieving latency statistics for discovered hosts\n8. Implement proper error handling and logging\n9. Include configuration options for scan parameters (timeout, retry count, parallel scan limit)\n10. Follow PenPal's plugin architecture patterns for consistency\n\nThe plugin should be optimized for performance, especially when scanning large networks, and should handle rate limiting to prevent network flooding.",
      "testStrategy": "Testing should verify both functionality and integration with PenPal's ecosystem:\n\n1. Unit tests:\n   - Test ICMP sweep functionality with mock network responses\n   - Verify proper parsing and handling of ICMP responses\n   - Test error handling for various failure scenarios\n\n2. Integration tests:\n   - Verify MQTT subscription and message handling\n   - Test ScanQueue integration for job submission\n   - Validate JobsTracker updates during scan lifecycle\n   - Confirm CoreAPI upsert operations work correctly\n\n3. End-to-end tests:\n   - Deploy the plugin in a test environment with known hosts\n   - Trigger a network discovery and verify hosts are correctly identified\n   - Test GraphQL queries to retrieve ping results and status\n   - Verify latency measurements are accurate using controlled test hosts\n\n4. Performance tests:\n   - Measure scan performance on networks of various sizes\n   - Test concurrent scanning capabilities\n   - Verify resource usage remains within acceptable limits\n\n5. Security tests:\n   - Ensure the plugin handles permissions correctly for ICMP operations\n   - Verify no sensitive information is exposed in logs or responses\n\nAll tests should be automated where possible and included in CI/CD pipeline.",
      "status": "done",
      "dependencies": [],
      "priority": "high",
      "subtasks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "title": "Implement Core ICMP Ping Sweep Functionality",
          "description": "Develop the core ICMP ping sweep engine that efficiently discovers hosts across network ranges using asynchronous, non-blocking operations for optimal performance.",
          "dependencies": [],
          "details": "1. Create a Python module using aioping or scapy for asynchronous ICMP operations\n2. Implement worker pool pattern with configurable concurrency limits\n3. Build network range parser to convert CIDR notation to individual IPs\n4. Add rate limiting mechanism to prevent network flooding\n5. Implement timeout and retry logic with configurable parameters\n6. Create structured result format with host status and latency metrics\n7. Add proper error handling for network issues and permission errors\n8. Test with various network sizes to verify performance\n9. Testing approach: Unit test individual components and perform integration tests against test networks of varying sizes",
          "status": "done",
          "parentTaskId": 32
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "title": "Containerize the Ping Plugin with Docker",
          "description": "Package the ICMP ping functionality into a Docker container with appropriate permissions and minimal footprint, following security best practices.",
          "dependencies": [
